%%DOC welcome
path: ReliefOS/Welcome
title: Welcome
author: ReliefOS Team
version: 1.0
%%BODY
# Welcome to ReliefOS Help

ReliefOS Help stores several Markdown pages inside one `.hlp` file. Use the tree on the left to choose a page, then read the document on the right.

## Navigation

- Use **Back** and **Forward** to jump between pages you have visited.
- The help language follows the application locale. Reopen Help after changing the system language.
- Use the mouse wheel to scroll long documents.

## Included topics

| Topic | Purpose |
| File permissions | Explains R, W, X, Delete and Manage |
| Blue screen errors | Explains STOP reasons, CPU vectors and Page Fault flags |
| Help file format | Shows how developers can write `.hlp` files |

%%ENDDOC

%%DOC system.bugcheck
path: System/Blue screen errors
title: Blue screen errors
author: ReliefOS Team
version: 1.0
%%BODY
# Blue screen errors

ReliefOS shows a blue bugcheck screen when the kernel cannot safely continue. The screen starts with a `STOP:` reason, then diagnostic fields such as `PID`, `TASK`, `MODE`, `VECTOR`, `ERROR`, `RIP`, `RSP`, `SS`, `CS`, `RFLAGS`, `CR2`, `TICKS` and `UPTIME_MS`.

## How to read the screen

| Field | Meaning |
| STOP | The primary reason. Match this first. |
| PID / TASK | The current task when the stop happened. |
| MODE | `kernel` means kernel or trusted system code; `user` means a Ring-3 context was active. |
| VECTOR | CPU exception vector number. `14` is Page Fault. |
| ERROR | CPU error code. For Page Fault, use the `PF FLAGS` line. |
| RIP / RSP | Instruction pointer and stack pointer at the fault. |
| CR2 | Faulting virtual address for Page Fault. |
| TICKS / UPTIME_MS | Time since boot, useful for matching serial logs. |

## STOP reasons

| STOP reason | Meaning | Usual next step |
| KERNEL PANIC | Kernel code called `panic()` or `bugcheck_panic()`. The detail line is the panic message. | Search the message in the source tree or serial log. |
| Unhandled Page Fault | A kernel-mode page fault, or a page fault that could not be recovered by lazy mapping. | Check `CR2`, `RIP`, and `PF FLAGS`. |
| Divide By Zero | CPU vector 0. Code divided by zero. | Inspect the function at `RIP`. |
| Debug Exception | CPU vector 1. Debug trap. | Usually a bad debug state or unexpected trap. |
| Non Maskable Interrupt | CPU vector 2. NMI. | Treat as hardware or emulator-level interruption. |
| Breakpoint | CPU vector 3. Breakpoint instruction reached. | Remove unintended breakpoint or inspect debug path. |
| Overflow | CPU vector 4. Overflow trap. | Inspect arithmetic path near `RIP`. |
| Bound Range Exceeded | CPU vector 5. Bound check failed. | Inspect array/range code. |
| Invalid Opcode | CPU vector 6. Unknown or unsupported instruction. | Check compiler output, CPU feature use, or corrupted code. |
| Device Not Available | CPU vector 7. FPU/SIMD device state not available. | Check task/FPU setup. |
| Double Fault | CPU vector 8. The CPU faulted while handling another fault. | Often stack, IDT, TSS or severe memory corruption. |
| Invalid TSS | CPU vector 10. Task-state segment is invalid. | Check TSS/GDT setup. |
| Segment Not Present | CPU vector 11. Segment descriptor is unavailable. | Check GDT/segment selector state. |
| Stack Segment Fault | CPU vector 12. Stack segment fault. | Check stack selector and stack pointer. |
| General Protection Fault | CPU vector 13. Protection rule violation. | Check bad selectors, privilege transitions, invalid descriptors or privileged instructions. |
| Page Fault | CPU vector 14. Memory translation or protection fault. | Decode `PF FLAGS` and `CR2`. |
| x87 Floating Point Fault | CPU vector 16. x87 floating-point exception. | Check floating-point state and exception masks. |
| Alignment Check | CPU vector 17. Alignment check exception. | Check unaligned memory access under alignment checking. |
| Machine Check | CPU vector 18. CPU reported a machine check. | Treat as hardware/emulator fatal state. |
| SIMD Floating Point Fault | CPU vector 19. SSE/SIMD floating-point exception. | Check SIMD state and exception masks. |
| Virtualization Exception | CPU vector 20. Virtualization exception. | Check VM/emulator or virtualization feature use. |
| Control Protection Exception | CPU vector 21. Control-flow protection exception. | Check control protection state if enabled. |
| Hypervisor Injection Exception | CPU vector 28. Hypervisor-injected exception. | Check hypervisor/emulator state. |
| VMM Communication Exception | CPU vector 29. VMM communication exception. | Check hypervisor communication path. |
| Security Exception | CPU vector 30. CPU security exception. | Check CPU security feature state. |
| CPU Exception | Any other vector. | Use `VECTOR`, `ERROR` and `RIP` to identify the source. |

## Page Fault flags

When `VECTOR` is `14`, the blue screen shows `PF FLAGS`.

| Flag | Meaning when 1 | Meaning when 0 |
| P | Protection violation on a present page. | Page was not present. |
| W | Fault happened during a write. | Fault happened during a read. |
| U | Fault came from user mode. | Fault came from supervisor/kernel mode. |
| RSVD | Reserved page-table bit was set. | No reserved-bit violation. |
| IF | Fault happened during instruction fetch. | Fault happened during data access. |

## User application faults

Unrecoverable user-mode page faults are delivered to the application as Linux signals, normally `SIGSEGV` or `SIGBUS`. The default signal action terminates the process. Kernel-mode faults without a safe recovery path still stop the system; inspect the serial diagnostics for the fault address and instruction pointer.

%%ENDDOC

%%DOC fs.permissions
path: System/File permissions
title: File permissions
author: ReliefOS Team
version: 1.0
%%BODY
# File permissions

ReliefOS uses a Windows-style permission model for files and directories. The current v1 permission bits are stored in ACL metadata and checked by kernel file operations.

## Permission bits

| Bit | Meaning |
| R / Read | Read a file, list a directory, or view basic metadata. |
| W / Write | Create files, write existing files, or modify directory contents. |
| X / Execute | Run an `.elf` file or traverse a directory while resolving a path. |
| Delete | Remove or rename the object. |
| Manage | Change permissions, repair ACL metadata, or take ownership when allowed. |

## Important rules

- Administrators can manage system-wide objects and repair broken ACL metadata.
- Normal users are expected to control their own `/home/<name>` files.
- Opening a shortcut checks permission on both the `.lnk` file and its target.
- Missing ACL metadata falls back to system default rules.

%%ENDDOC

%%DOC help.format
path: Developers/Help file format
title: Help file format
author: ReliefOS Team
version: 1.0
%%BODY
# Help file format

A `.hlp` file is a plain text container. It starts with file metadata, then one or more document pages.

```text
    %%HLP
    title: My Help
    author: Example
    version: 1.0

    %%DOC page.id
    path: Section/Page title
    title: Page title
    author: Example
    version: 1.0
    %%BODY
    # Markdown page
    Body text.
    %%ENDDOC
```

Document ids may contain letters, digits, `.`, `_` and `-`. The `path` field builds the left-side tree. Store translated files in `/usr/share/doc/reliefos/<locale>/`; the default file is the English fallback.

%%ENDDOC
